[should artifact gifts be bounded or unbounded?]

> > If you could play every class
> > and always end up with <insert my favorite blessed fooproof +7
> > artifact here>, then every class ends up being (mostly) the same.
> 
> Yes, but of course, if you have to play every class with a blessed
> fooproof +7 "I only got one altar all game, so I have to use this artifact
> even though it's totally wrong for this character" weapon it's even less
> interesting.

A +7 crysknife or silver saber is a perfectly viable ascension weapon.
You don't have to use an artifact just because it's an artifact.

> Because it's so hard to get artifacts now, you
> haven't made it so people will use the weapons because they're interesting
> and fit the class well, rather, that they'll use them because they're the
> only weapons they can get.  And that is a Bad Thing (TM).

If you really want a particular artifact to support your character
set-up, then that's what wishes are for. In general, having to make
the best use of what you're given is a Good Thing (TM).

> > I just don't want to encourage people to stick around on
> > the same level.  I like Dylan's quote on this: "Maybe the gods are as
> > bored with sac-fests as I am."
> 
> People will still do sac-fests for the other benefits.

You think? Most people, from what I see, sacrifice for these reasons:

a) primarily, in the hope of getting the Nifty Artifact of choice
b) to improve their luck (which can be done in other ways, and once
   you're at max with a luckstone, you're done)
c) to mollify their god's anger (special case)
d) because the altar and the corpse happen to be handy

Of these, only a) and b) involve sac-fests; take a) away, and people
are encouraged to get on down the dungeon, not hang around waiting for
the perfect result.

